  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Afghanistan - Floods</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">140,000 people The response targets vulnerable households, including those headed by women (particularly widows), households with people with disabilities, and families that have lost homes or agricultural assets. Additional support is prioritized for women, children, and people with specific health and psychosocial needs.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Assistance is focused on the 14 most severely affected provinces, including Badakhshan, Baghlan, Ghor, and Uruzgan, with Baghlan as a priority. The operation also includes plans for winterization, recognizing the climate risks and the need for continued resilience-building in these areas.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The operation aims to assist 140,000 people directly affected by floods across 14 provinces of Afghanistan, addressing urgent needs such as shelter, food, WASH (Water, Sanitation, and Hygiene), healthcare, and livelihoods, with a focus on recovery and resilience.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The strategy involves distributing cash, essential household items, and shelter support; deploying mobile health teams; providing access to clean water and sanitation; and strengthening livelihoods. Volunteers and local staff are central to community engagement and disaster preparedness, while the IFRC will coordinate with ARCS and other partners for effective response. There is also a focus on training local masons and communities in safer construction techniques to enhance disaster resilience.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This intervention is crucial as the floods have devastated communities, with extensive loss of life, homes, and livelihoods. Agricultural lands, food stocks, and critical infrastructure like bridges and health facilities have been destroyed. The floods have increased vulnerability to health risks, economic hardship, and social instability.</narrative>
  </description>
